description:
Three navigation/interaction behaviors differ from upstream.

## 1. Circular wrap-around (up/down at boundaries)

**Upstream** (TreeList.handleInput):
```typescript
if (kb.matches(keyData, "tui.select.up")) {
  this.selectedIndex = this.selectedIndex === 0
    ? this.filteredNodes.length - 1   // wrap to last
    : this.selectedIndex - 1;
}
if (kb.matches(keyData, "tui.select.down")) {
  this.selectedIndex = this.selectedIndex === this.filteredNodes.length - 1
    ? 0   // wrap to first
    : this.selectedIndex + 1;
}
```

**Elixir** (move_cursor/2):
```elixir
defp move_cursor(%__MODULE__{selected_index: idx, filtered_nodes: nodes} = state, delta) do
  count = length(nodes)
  new_idx = (idx + delta) |> max(0) |> min(count - 1)   # clamps, no wrap
  %{state | selected_index: new_idx}
end
```

Fix: replace `max/min` clamp with modular wrap in move_cursor/2 for delta = ±1 (page up/down
should still clamp, not wrap, as upstream does).

## 2. ctrl+left / ctrl+right as additional page-up / page-down

**Upstream** (TreeList.handleInput):
```typescript
} else if (kb.matches(keyData, "tui.editor.cursorLeft") || kb.matches(keyData, "tui.select.pageUp")) {
  // Page up
  this.selectedIndex = Math.max(0, this.selectedIndex - this.maxVisibleLines);
} else if (kb.matches(keyData, "tui.editor.cursorRight") || kb.matches(keyData, "tui.select.pageDown")) {
  // Page down
  this.selectedIndex = Math.min(this.filteredNodes.length - 1, this.selectedIndex + this.maxVisibleLines);
}
```
Note: tui.editor.cursorLeft defaults to ctrl+left and alt+left; cursorRight to ctrl+right and alt+right.

**Elixir**: only tui.select.pageUp / tui.select.pageDown are handled. ctrl+left/right are not mapped
in the tree context (they are used for fold/branch navigation as app.tree.foldOrUp/foldOrDown — see
opi-iu9.3 for folding). NOTE: folding (opi-iu9.3) takes priority over page-up/page-down for
ctrl+left/right. When folding is not applicable (node is not foldable and not folded), the keys
fall through to the segment-jump behavior in upstream — NOT page-up/down. The page-up/down via
tui.editor.cursorLeft/Right is only when the node is neither foldable nor folded.

Actually re-reading upstream more carefully, the cursor-left/right for page up/down is a SEPARATE
branch in handleInput — it runs AFTER the foldOrUp/foldOrDown check. The foldOrUp handler uses
kb.matches(keyData, "app.tree.foldOrUp") which maps to ctrl+left/alt+left. The pageUp via
cursorLeft also maps to ctrl+left. In upstream these are distinct keybinding IDs but share default
keys — the foldOrUp branch runs first, and on a non-foldable/non-folded node it jumps to segment
start. The cursorLeft page-up is in a later else-if. So ctrl+left is NEVER a page-up key when
foldOrUp is handled first. This appears to be dead code for the ctrl+left mapping — but
tui.select.pageUp (pageUp key) still works. The alt+left version is also foldOrUp.

Fix: No change needed for ctrl+left/right once opi-iu9.3 (folding) lands — the pageUp key
(tui.select.pageUp, default: pageUp) still works for page navigation. However Elixir should
verify it handles tui.select.pageUp correctly (it does). If the leftover cursorLeft/pageUp
dual-binding matters, it is a very minor nit.

Revised scope for this ticket: only items 1 and 3.

## 3. Escape when search is active should clear search, not close overlay

**Upstream** (TreeList.handleInput):
```typescript
} else if (kb.matches(keyData, "tui.select.cancel")) {
  if (this.searchQuery) {
    this.searchQuery = "";
    this.foldedNodes.clear();
    this.applyFilter();
  } else {
    this.onCancel?.();  // only cancel when query is empty
  }
}
```

**Elixir** (handle_key/2):
```elixir
Keybindings.matches?(kb, key, "tui.select.cancel") -> {state, [:cancel]}
```
Always emits :cancel, regardless of whether a search query is active.

Fix: in handle_key/2, check if state.search_query is non-empty (after opi-ce3.3 lands);
if so, clear the query and re-filter rather than emitting :cancel. When search_query is
empty (or opi-ce3.3 not yet landed), emit :cancel as today.

This ticket depends on opi-ce3.3 (search_query field added to struct).

## Upstream references
- handleInput() wrap-around lines 900-910 in tree-selector.ts
- handleInput() escape/cancel branch lines 940-949 in tree-selector.ts

## Verification
1. Open /tree, press down at the last entry — confirm cursor wraps to the first entry.
   Press up at the first entry — confirm cursor wraps to the last entry.
2. Open /tree, type a search query, press Escape — confirm search is cleared (entries show
   again) but overlay remains open. Press Escape again — overlay closes.
